# sky130A — open reference extraction deck for vyges-extract
#
# Capacitance is calibrated against the foundry-reference extractor (OpenROAD **OpenRCX**, nom
# corner, rules.openrcx.sky130A.nom) across a **set of 14 sky130 blocks**, each scored against the
# SPEF its own OpenLane run produced. `fft_ctrl_tlul` (14 238 nets) was **held out of every fit**
# and scored only afterwards:
#
#     ground 0.98x   coupling 1.00x   TOTAL 0.99x
#     per-net median: ground 0.96 (p10 0.77, p90 1.20), coupling 1.00 (p10 0.89, p90 1.13)
#
# Fitted by `correlation/calibrate_coupling.py`, then ground by `correlation/calibrate_ground.py`,
# in that order — `shield_k` multiplies OUR coupling, so it cannot be fitted until the coupling
# column is trustworthy, or you are fitting one error against another.
#
# ── 2026-08-06: the coupling column halved, and now means what its name says ────────────
# This deck previously carried met1 = 0.21941 fF/um, ~2x any value a single sidewall pair can
# have, and said so in this header. The cause was NOT the model. OpenRCX writes every coupling
# cap into BOTH nets' SPEF blocks (`extSpef::writeSrcCouplingCaps` / `writeTgtCouplingCaps`), and
# our correlation harness credited every listing — so every reference coupling value the deck was
# ever fitted against was doubled, and the deck doubled with it. Ratios looked right the whole
# time because both sides were inflated equally.
#
# The harness now counts each cap once (`correlation/decompose.py`), and two mechanisms were
# corrected in the engine at the same time (see `README.md` → Known limits):
#
#   * the fall-off is the layer's CHARACTERISED curve, not 1/s — see `couple_shape` below;
#   * a wire couples only to what it can SEE, with shadowing resolved run-length by run-length,
#     and the power grid taking part as metal that blocks but never couples.
#
# The result is a coupling column that lands on the reference's own characterised values:
#
#     layer   this deck   OpenRCX characterised   ratio
#     met1      0.09320          0.1055           0.88x
#     met2      0.10917          0.1045           1.04x
#     met3      0.11205          0.1103           1.02x
#
# ── shield_k — why ground is not just a per-um number ──────────────────────────────────
# Field that terminates on a neighbour is field that did not terminate on ground, so a net's
# grounded cap is reduced by `shield_k * Cc_net`. Without it a deck fitted on a sparse block
# over-states ground on a dense one — an earlier deck tracked `counter` to 0.997 and ran 1.37x
# over on a real block, with a +0.94 correlation between a net's coupling-to-ground ratio and its
# error. `Cc_net` counts a net's coupling from BOTH sides, matching `engine.rs`.
#
# `shield_k` is carried over from the previous calibration, DOUBLED, so the amount subtracted is
# unchanged now that the coupling column has halved. Re-fitting it from scratch was tried and
# scored worse on the held-out block (ground total 0.89x against 0.98x), so the algebraic carry
# is what ships. The ground columns are unchanged from the previous calibration.
#
# ── Unconstrained layers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# li1 (0 constraining nets — sky130 routing uses li1 only for via landings) and met5 (22) cannot
# be fitted from this corpus. They carry OpenRCX's own characterised value for that layer rather
# than a neighbouring layer's fitted one; met2 and met3 land within 4% of characterised, so that
# is a defensible prior. met4 IS fitted, but on only 1 659 nets, and sits at 1.48x characterised —
# treat it as thin rather than settled.
#
# ── Known bound: cross-layer (diagonal) coupling is not modelled ────────────────────────
# The reference reports 11 937 pairs on the held-out block that we do not, worth 637 fF — 2.9% of
# its coupling. That is its DIAGUNDER mechanism: with `DIAGMODEL ON`, OpenRCX books field between
# a wire and same-direction wires on OTHER layers as real coupling. This deck defines no
# `interlayer` coefficients, and our `interlayer` term is an areal-overlap model that cannot
# represent a diagonal-distance one — fitting it jointly produces a ~25x parallel-plate
# coefficient, which is curve-fitting, not mechanism. Stated, not hidden.
#
# ── Resistance ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Physical, from the sky130_fd_sc_hd nom tech LEF (RESISTANCE RPERSQ). Not calibrated against
# anything; an R correlation pass is separate.
#
# Calibrated to OpenRCX, a reference extractor — NOT to silicon. A sign-off / certified per-fab
# deck is silicon-correlated and NDA, and is never in this repo.
#
# layer  res(ohm/um)  cap(fF/um)  coupling(fF/um)  s_ref(um)
li1      12.8         0.060        0.0795           0.17
met1      0.125       0.12494      0.09320          0.14
met2      0.125       0.09676      0.10917          0.14
met3      0.047       0.13365      0.11205          0.30
met4      0.047       0.13365      0.16041          0.30
met5      0.0285      0.13365      0.0741           1.60
via       9.3

# ── couple_shape — the characterised fall-off ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Coupling does NOT fall off as 1/s. Measured from the reference deck's own DIST tables, met1
# keeps 0.773 of its minimum-spacing coupling at twice the spacing where 1/s gives 0.500. Each
# curve below is that layer's table, normalised to 1.0 at its first characterised sample, so it
# scales the `coupling` column rather than replacing it. Interpolation follows the reference's own
# `extDistRCTable::getComputeRC`: clamp below the first sample, linear between, 1/gap past the end.
couple_shape li1  0.17:1.0000 0.34:0.6219 0.425:0.5414 0.51:0.4791 0.595:0.4275 0.68:0.3843 0.765:0.3481 0.85:0.3176 1.02:0.2697 1.36:0.2069 1.7:0.1688
couple_shape met1 0.14:1.0000 0.28:0.7734 0.35:0.6550 0.42:0.6001 0.49:0.5448 0.56:0.4892 0.63:0.4376 0.7:0.4157 0.84:0.3715 1.12:0.2860 1.4:0.2500
couple_shape met2 0.14:1.0000 0.28:0.7667 0.35:0.6513 0.42:0.5984 0.49:0.5451 0.56:0.4915 0.63:0.4419 0.7:0.4210 0.84:0.3790 1.12:0.2975 1.4:0.2626
couple_shape met3 0.3:1.0000 0.6:0.7179 0.75:0.6595 0.9:0.6007 1.05:0.5415 1.2:0.4820 1.35:0.4220 1.5:0.3888 1.8:0.3493 2.4:0.2696 3:0.2298
couple_shape met4 0.3:1.0000 0.6:0.7228 0.75:0.6668 0.9:0.6104 1.05:0.5535 1.2:0.4963 1.35:0.4387 1.5:0.4067 1.8:0.3685 2.4:0.2915 3:0.2517
couple_shape met5 1.6:1.0000 3.2:0.5282 4:0.4311 4.8:0.3657 5.6:0.3181 6.4:0.2823 7.2:0.2544 8:0.2319 9.6:0.1976 12.8:0.1545 16:0.1285

couple_cutoff 2.0     # um — ignore lateral coupling beyond this gap
shield_k  0.36162     # grounded cap -= shield_k * Cc_net (both sides) — see above
